WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.550
Welcome to Day 3 of Case Management Training with Legal Support Help.

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:09.460
Today we cover two phases of the case: first lien negotiations and reduction,

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:10.763
and then disbursement.

00:00:11.113 --> 00:00:14.979
Throughout the day, our sample case is John Doe versus Robert Smith,

00:00:14.979 --> 00:00:17.707
the same file you're working on in the platform.

00:00:18.057 --> 00:00:19.590
Let's start with liens.

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:23.307
Once a case settles, lienholders want to be paid in full.

00:00:23.657 --> 00:00:27.569
But the attorney has legal arguments that can push them to accept less,

00:00:27.569 --> 00:00:30.158
and every dollar saved goes back to the client.

00:00:30.508 --> 00:00:34.620
The Common Fund Doctrine says a lienholder collecting from money our attorney worked

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:37.068
to recover should share the cost of recovering it.

00:00:37.418 --> 00:00:40.042
That's exactly what happened on our file.

00:00:40.392 --> 00:00:45.950
Peach State Health Plan asserted 1,860 dollars and accepted 1,240 dollars,

00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:48.879
a one third procurement cost reduction.

00:00:49.229 --> 00:00:52.428
The Made Whole Doctrine says that in many jurisdictions,

00:00:52.428 --> 00:00:56.313
a lienholder collects nothing until the client is fully compensated,

00:00:56.313 --> 00:00:57.913
pain and suffering included.

00:00:58.263 --> 00:01:03.159
And comparative fault cuts a lien by the same share the client's recovery was cut,

00:01:03.159 --> 00:01:05.368
the slide's example being 20 percent.

00:01:05.718 --> 00:01:07.529
You can name these doctrines.

00:01:07.879 --> 00:01:10.793
Which one to argue is always Atty. Thorne's call.

00:01:13.343 --> 00:01:16.083
Not every lien comes from a medical provider.

00:01:16.433 --> 00:01:19.440
Sometimes a lawyer who came before us wants to be paid.

00:01:19.790 --> 00:01:22.612
Quantum Meruit means as much as he has deserved.

00:01:22.962 --> 00:01:26.352
It's how the law values work when there's no valid contract,

00:01:26.352 --> 00:01:29.800
typically when a lawyer is dismissed before the case settles.

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:34.323
Personal injury fees are usually contingent, a percentage of the win,

00:01:34.323 --> 00:01:37.348
so a dismissed lawyer can't claim that percentage.

00:01:37.698 --> 00:01:41.123
Instead, they file a lien for the work actually done.

00:01:41.473 --> 00:01:45.484
The law balances two rights: the client can fire their lawyer at any time,

00:01:45.484 --> 00:01:48.358
and the lawyer deserves pay for the value they added.

00:01:48.708 --> 00:01:52.400
Our file shows why you check the facts behind any such claim.

00:01:52.749 --> 00:01:59.943
On October 14th, 2026, Vance and Kettering Injury Law sent a charging lien demanding

00:01:59.943 --> 00:02:03.454
half the fee, 6,666 dollars and 67 cents.

00:02:03.804 --> 00:02:06.810
But the agreement was signed by John's mother, not John,

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:10.513
and their own time record showed an intake call and a welcome letter,

00:02:10.513 --> 00:02:11.211
nothing more.

00:02:11.561 --> 00:02:14.512
It was logged and routed to Atty. Thorne the same day,

00:02:14.512 --> 00:02:17.354
and on his instruction those funds were put on hold.

00:02:17.704 --> 00:02:23.428
On October 19th, 2026, they withdrew the lien in writing and claimed no fee.

00:02:25.978 --> 00:02:27.998
Here's the mindset for this phase.

00:02:28.348 --> 00:02:32.089
Half of a case manager's job is getting money from the insurance company,

00:02:32.089 --> 00:02:34.908
and the other half is protecting it from the providers.

00:02:35.258 --> 00:02:38.613
The pro rata argument shows a provider that the pie is fixed.

00:02:38.963 --> 00:02:42.553
If they won't come down, the slide's example is 30 percent,

00:02:42.553 --> 00:02:46.022
the settlement may stall, and nobody gets paid for years.

00:02:46.372 --> 00:02:48.334
Our file makes the math real.

00:02:48.684 --> 00:02:53.386
Had John recovered only Keystone's 10,000 dollar BI limit,

00:02:53.386 --> 00:02:59.629
after the fee and costs just 6,179 dollars and 92 cents would have been left,

00:02:59.629 --> 00:03:02.953
against 7,965 dollars of unreduced liens.

00:03:03.303 --> 00:03:06.051
Shared pro rata, the liens would have taken all of it,

00:03:06.051 --> 00:03:08.341
and John would have walked away with nothing.

00:03:08.691 --> 00:03:10.537
That's why this lesson matters.

00:03:10.887 --> 00:03:14.568
The Made Whole argument applies only where state law allows.

00:03:14.918 --> 00:03:19.295
Peach State is a fully insured plan, so state law governs its reduction,

00:03:19.295 --> 00:03:21.849
and that analysis belongs to Atty. Thorne.

00:03:22.199 --> 00:03:26.496
And never send a check without a signed payoff letter stating it's full and final

00:03:26.496 --> 00:03:27.876
satisfaction of all liens.

00:03:28.226 --> 00:03:32.502
Before Riverton Spine and Rehab's reduced 4,580 dollars goes out,

00:03:32.502 --> 00:03:34.739
that letter has to be in the file.

00:03:37.289 --> 00:03:40.760
Before you ask anyone for a discount, make sure the bill is right.

00:03:41.110 --> 00:03:45.186
Some providers upcode, billing a more complex service than they gave,

00:03:45.186 --> 00:03:48.494
or unbundle, splitting one service into several charges.

00:03:48.844 --> 00:03:52.351
If the bill is wrong, you're not negotiating, you're correcting.

00:03:52.701 --> 00:03:55.684
So cross reference the records against the billing statement.

00:03:56.034 --> 00:03:59.749
The slide's example is a Level 3 visit billed as a Level 5.

00:04:00.099 --> 00:04:03.373
That's a correction you demand, not a favor you ask for.

00:04:03.723 --> 00:04:05.314
Then hunt for duplicates.

00:04:05.664 --> 00:04:07.301
Our file has a real one.

00:04:07.651 --> 00:04:12.769
Riverton Spine and Rehab billed 34 lines at 185 dollars each,

00:04:12.769 --> 00:04:17.299
6,290 dollars, but the attendance log shows 33 visits.

00:04:17.649 --> 00:04:20.795
May 4th, 2026 was billed twice.

00:04:21.145 --> 00:04:27.315
The audit took the bill to 6,105 dollars, and only then did negotiation start,

00:04:27.315 --> 00:04:29.214
ending at 4,580 dollars.

00:04:29.564 --> 00:04:32.873
Once the bill is clean, bring in the Common Fund Doctrine.

00:04:33.223 --> 00:04:36.799
The attorney did the work and paid the costs that created the money.

00:04:37.149 --> 00:04:41.355
Under the one third rule, lienholders in many states reduce their claim by their

00:04:41.355 --> 00:04:42.617
share of fees and costs.

00:04:42.967 --> 00:04:48.087
That's how Peach State's 1,860 dollars became 1,240.

00:04:50.637 --> 00:04:53.423
Now the tool that ties it all together: the net sheet.

00:04:53.773 --> 00:04:57.324
When a case recovers from both BI and UM, as ours does,

00:04:57.324 --> 00:05:00.228
we use one combined sheet, for three reasons.

00:05:00.578 --> 00:05:02.424
First, avoiding double dipping.

00:05:02.774 --> 00:05:07.990
Carriers audit our files, so we show exactly where every dollar came from and pay

00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:09.729
liens without mixing funds.

00:05:10.079 --> 00:05:15.027
On our file, the BI stream is 10,000 dollars and the UM stream is 30,000,

00:05:15.027 --> 00:05:20.586
and costs and liens are allocated to each stream in proportion to its share of the

00:05:20.586 --> 00:05:22.281
gross, 25 and 75 percent.

00:05:22.631 --> 00:05:24.488
Second, fee transparency.

00:05:24.838 --> 00:05:30.137
The fee can be structured differently for a third party BI policy than a first party

00:05:30.137 --> 00:05:32.849
UM/UIM policy, so we track each separately.

00:05:33.199 --> 00:05:37.518
On John's file, the retainer's one third pre-suit fee applies to both.

00:05:37.868 --> 00:05:40.271
Third, protecting the final aggregate net.

00:05:40.621 --> 00:05:45.792
Side by side, the client sees how his coverages stacked up and how negotiation grew

00:05:45.792 --> 00:05:46.415
his check.

00:05:46.765 --> 00:05:55.038
For John, that's 20,359 dollars and 92 cents: 5,089 dollars and 98 cents from BI and

00:05:55.038 --> 00:05:58.584
15,269 dollars and 94 cents from UM.

00:06:01.134 --> 00:06:02.608
So what are the two streams?

00:06:02.958 --> 00:06:05.853
Stream one is third party Bodily Injury, or BI,

00:06:05.853 --> 00:06:08.194
paid by the at fault driver's insurer.

00:06:08.544 --> 00:06:12.283
The slide names GEICO or Progressive as general examples.

00:06:12.633 --> 00:06:16.300
On our file, it's Keystone Mutual, Robert Smith's carrier,

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:18.008
with a 10,000 dollar limit.

00:06:18.358 --> 00:06:22.425
Your focus is exhausting that limit first, because that's usually a legal

00:06:22.425 --> 00:06:25.266
prerequisite before the first party coverage opens.

00:06:25.616 --> 00:06:29.563
Keystone tendered its limit on August 12th, 2026.

00:06:29.913 --> 00:06:35.521
Stream two is first party Uninsured or Underinsured Motorist coverage, UM/UIM.

00:06:35.871 --> 00:06:40.271
The slide points to the client's own policy or a resident relative's household

00:06:40.271 --> 00:06:40.666
policy.

00:06:41.016 --> 00:06:46.530
John owns no car and has no policy of his own, so his UM comes from two places.

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:51.822
As a passenger, he reaches Harbor Point's 20,000 dollar Host UM on Kevin Alvarez's

00:06:51.822 --> 00:06:52.244
Escape.

00:06:52.594 --> 00:06:56.253
And as a resident relative of his mother, Margaret Doe,

00:06:56.253 --> 00:06:59.845
he reaches Cardinal State's 10,000 dollar Relative UM,

00:06:59.845 --> 00:07:01.708
which stacks behind Host UM.

00:07:02.058 --> 00:07:07.192
UM triggers only when BI is exhausted, or when the tortfeasor has no insurance at

00:07:07.192 --> 00:07:07.445
all.

00:07:07.795 --> 00:07:11.905
Order matters: BI, then Host UM, then Relative UM.

00:07:14.455 --> 00:07:18.867
Here's what the net sheet looks like: our LSH corporate net sheet.

00:07:19.217 --> 00:07:21.933
It reads top to bottom, the way the money flows.

00:07:22.283 --> 00:07:26.848
The slide image is a blank template, and Progressive Corporation is just its example

00:07:26.848 --> 00:07:27.392
reference.

00:07:27.742 --> 00:07:29.657
Let's fill it in with our file.

00:07:30.007 --> 00:07:34.763
Section one, the settlement recovery summary: a gross of 40,000 dollars from

00:07:34.763 --> 00:07:37.391
Keystone, Harbor Point and Cardinal State.

00:07:37.741 --> 00:07:41.422
Section two, attorney fees and advanced case expenses.

00:07:41.772 --> 00:07:44.628
The template shows 33.33 percent.

00:07:44.978 --> 00:07:50.330
John's fee is one third, 13,333 dollars and 33 cents.

00:07:50.680 --> 00:07:56.420
His case costs total 486 dollars and 75 cents, including the police report,

00:07:56.420 --> 00:08:01.319
medical records and billing, certified mail, MRI images on disc,

00:08:01.319 --> 00:08:03.079
and postage and copies.

00:08:03.429 --> 00:08:07.091
Every line on the template, from filing fees to file storage,

00:08:07.091 --> 00:08:09.072
gets checked, even if it's empty.

00:08:09.422 --> 00:08:13.926
Section three, the medical provider ledger, is where your lien work lands.

00:08:14.276 --> 00:08:18.884
For each provider you track charges, PIP, health and med pay payments,

00:08:18.884 --> 00:08:21.846
adjustments, balance owed and your max offer.

00:08:22.196 --> 00:08:25.914
John's MRI and orthopedic visits were paid by PIP,

00:08:25.914 --> 00:08:30.969
while Riverton Spine and Rehab's chiro and PT, treated under an LOP,

00:08:30.969 --> 00:08:33.423
carry the balance you negotiated.

00:08:35.973 --> 00:08:40.210
When a case has both BI and UM, the order of steps is everything.

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:44.508
First, secure the BI tender in writing from the at fault carrier.

00:08:44.858 --> 00:08:49.641
Keystone tendered its 10,000 dollar limit on August 12th, 2026.

00:08:49.991 --> 00:08:55.392
Second, before John signs any BI release, send a formal Consent to Settle to the UM

00:08:55.392 --> 00:08:58.385
carriers, to protect their subrogation rights.

00:08:58.735 --> 00:09:02.888
We sent requests to Harbor Point and Cardinal State on August 13th,

00:09:02.888 --> 00:09:06.049
and their consents came in on August 24th and 26th.

00:09:06.399 --> 00:09:11.415
Only then was the Limited Release signed, on August 28th, 2026.

00:09:11.765 --> 00:09:15.759
Settling BI without those consents could have cost John the whole UM stack.

00:09:16.109 --> 00:09:20.107
Third, negotiate medical bills once, against the combined value,

00:09:20.107 --> 00:09:22.668
not provider by provider for each policy.

00:09:23.018 --> 00:09:26.292
The slide's 50,000 dollar figure is a general example.

00:09:26.642 --> 00:09:31.475
Our stack is 40,000 dollars, and that's what Peach State and Riverton Spine and

00:09:31.475 --> 00:09:33.005
Rehab negotiated against.

00:09:33.354 --> 00:09:37.921
Fourth, fill out both sides of the net sheet together and confirm the client's net

00:09:37.921 --> 00:09:39.926
matches our software ledger exactly.

00:09:40.276 --> 00:09:45.152
On our file, that's 20,359 dollars and 92 cents.

00:09:47.702 --> 00:09:50.581
If you take one thing from this section, take this.

00:09:50.931 --> 00:09:54.077
Your job isn't data entry, it's financial engineering.

00:09:54.427 --> 00:09:58.593
When you pair a third party BI recovery with first party UM coverage,

00:09:58.593 --> 00:10:01.370
you grow the pool the client can recover from.

00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:03.380
The slide calls it doubling.

00:10:03.730 --> 00:10:09.212
On John's file, two UM layers added 30,000 dollars on top of a 10,000 dollar BI

00:10:09.212 --> 00:10:09.628
limit.

00:10:09.978 --> 00:10:13.944
Tracking those streams cleanly on one dual net sheet protects the firm,

00:10:13.944 --> 00:10:18.189
keeps the medical liens clear, and gets the client every dollar he's legally

00:10:18.189 --> 00:10:18.860
entitled to.

00:10:19.210 --> 00:10:20.673
Then there are firm costs.

00:10:21.023 --> 00:10:26.149
The slide lists police report fees, medical records retrieval and expert witness

00:10:26.149 --> 00:10:27.559
retainers as examples.

00:10:27.909 --> 00:10:34.035
John's costs were 486 dollars and 75 cents, including a 25 dollar police report and

00:10:34.035 --> 00:10:38.242
214 dollars and 75 cents for medical records and billing.

00:10:38.592 --> 00:10:42.955
Costs are totaled for the whole case, then taken out as one line or split

00:10:42.955 --> 00:10:43.851
proportionally.

00:10:44.201 --> 00:10:49.804
We split ours by share of gross: 121 dollars and 69 cents to BI,

00:10:49.804 --> 00:10:52.781
and 365 dollars and 6 cents to UM.

00:10:53.131 --> 00:10:56.428
Now, with the money calculated, let's get it out the door.

00:10:58.978 --> 00:11:01.846
Which brings us to our second phase, disbursement.

00:11:02.196 --> 00:11:06.224
This is where money leaves the trust account, so mistakes are hard to undo.

00:11:06.574 --> 00:11:11.578
On our file, disbursement happened on October 22nd, 2026.

00:11:11.928 --> 00:11:13.774
Keep four objectives in mind.

00:11:14.124 --> 00:11:17.143
Accuracy means every payment matches what was approved,

00:11:17.143 --> 00:11:19.558
the figures on the net sheet you just built.

00:11:19.908 --> 00:11:24.645
Compliance means following every local, state and federal regulation that applies.

00:11:24.994 --> 00:11:29.406
Fraud prevention means spotting red flags before funds go out, not after.

00:11:29.756 --> 00:11:31.800
Remember the Vance and Kettering lien.

00:11:32.150 --> 00:11:35.243
A claim that didn't add up arrived days before disbursement,

00:11:35.243 --> 00:11:38.698
and the funds it targeted were held until Atty. Thorne resolved it.

00:11:39.048 --> 00:11:43.852
The slide then lists compliance a second time, about meeting statutory requirements

00:11:43.852 --> 00:11:47.267
like the Statute of Limitations before the demand went out.

00:11:47.617 --> 00:11:52.059
On John's file, the demand went to Keystone on July 20th,

00:11:52.059 --> 00:11:57.201
2026, well ahead of the limitation date docketed for February 6th,

00:11:57.201 --> 00:11:57.590
2028.

00:11:57.940 --> 00:12:02.405
Disbursement is your last checkpoint, so confirm that everything that should have

00:12:02.405 --> 00:12:04.721
been done earlier is actually in the file.

00:12:07.271 --> 00:12:10.231
So how does a payment actually move through the office?

00:12:10.581 --> 00:12:13.100
Think of four stages, each feeding the next.

00:12:13.450 --> 00:12:15.331
Verification comes first.

00:12:15.681 --> 00:12:19.876
You pull files marked Ready for Payment and confirm every condition precedent is

00:12:19.876 --> 00:12:23.704
met, meaning everything that must happen before money moves has happened.

00:12:24.054 --> 00:12:29.111
On our file, that meant all three carrier checks had been received and every lien

00:12:29.111 --> 00:12:33.981
was resolved, including the Vance and Kettering hold released on October 19th,

00:12:33.981 --> 00:12:34.294
2026.

00:12:34.644 --> 00:12:36.327
Next is documentation.

00:12:36.677 --> 00:12:40.891
Gather invoices and receipts and make sure they're readable and match the claim.

00:12:41.241 --> 00:12:42.507
Then authorization.

00:12:42.857 --> 00:12:47.060
The release of funds is signed off, and the packet goes to finance and accounting.

00:12:47.410 --> 00:12:52.415
Our funds cleared on October 20th, and John signed his Settlement Statement on

00:12:52.415 --> 00:12:53.250
October 21st.

00:12:53.600 --> 00:12:55.364
Finally, reconciliation.

00:12:55.714 --> 00:12:59.963
Once you confirm the funds were received, update the case file to show Paid.

00:13:00.313 --> 00:13:06.560
After John's disbursement on October 22nd, 2026, the trust ledger went to zero.

00:13:06.910 --> 00:13:11.168
That last step keeps the whole team looking at the same accurate picture of the

00:13:11.168 --> 00:13:11.437
case.

00:13:11.787 --> 00:13:15.456
Next, let's look at what goes into that documentation packet.

00:13:18.006 --> 00:13:21.280
Here's what belongs in the packet before money is released.

00:13:21.630 --> 00:13:23.848
Each item answers a simple question.

00:13:24.198 --> 00:13:25.231
Who's getting paid?

00:13:25.581 --> 00:13:28.948
That's proof of identity, a valid ID for the payee.

00:13:29.298 --> 00:13:30.726
Who asked for the payment?

00:13:31.076 --> 00:13:35.130
That's the payment request form, signed by the beneficiary or an authorized

00:13:35.130 --> 00:13:35.940
representative.

00:13:36.290 --> 00:13:38.183
What exactly are we paying for?

00:13:38.533 --> 00:13:43.815
That's the verified invoices, with dates, service descriptions and tax IDs.

00:13:44.165 --> 00:13:49.719
On our file, Riverton Spine and Rehab's invoice has to match what was audited and

00:13:49.719 --> 00:13:52.873
agreed, not the 6,290 dollars it first billed.

00:13:53.223 --> 00:13:54.523
Where is the money going?

00:13:54.873 --> 00:13:59.292
That's the banking information, and the slide pairs it with updating the case file

00:13:59.292 --> 00:14:01.340
to Paid once the payment goes through.

00:14:01.690 --> 00:14:06.903
And finally, W-9 and other tax forms where they apply, which is for vendor payments.

00:14:07.253 --> 00:14:10.410
If any one of these is missing, the packet isn't ready.

00:14:10.760 --> 00:14:15.087
It's far easier to hold a payment for a missing document than to pull back money

00:14:15.087 --> 00:14:17.088
that already went to the wrong place.

00:14:17.438 --> 00:14:20.015
Which brings us to the challenges you'll run into.

00:14:22.565 --> 00:14:26.489
Even with a good checklist, a few problems come up again and again.

00:14:26.839 --> 00:14:28.743
The first is incomplete paperwork.

00:14:29.093 --> 00:14:30.835
Use a Kickback Protocol.

00:14:31.185 --> 00:14:34.273
Tell the client exactly what's missing and give a deadline.

00:14:34.623 --> 00:14:39.007
A vague request just leaves the file sitting while the client waits for money that's

00:14:39.007 --> 00:14:39.894
already been won.

00:14:40.244 --> 00:14:44.409
The second is a change of circumstance, when something about the payment changes

00:14:44.409 --> 00:14:46.908
along the way, like a new address or payee name.

00:14:47.258 --> 00:14:51.205
Per the slide, that needs a signature from the beneficiary or an authorized

00:14:51.205 --> 00:14:51.995
representative.

00:14:52.345 --> 00:14:56.822
On our file, Keystone's adjuster asked whether Peach State should be added as a

00:14:56.822 --> 00:14:58.069
payee on the BI check.

00:14:58.419 --> 00:15:01.553
That went to Atty. Thorne, and the payee stayed unchanged.

00:15:01.903 --> 00:15:04.481
Changes like that are never yours to decide alone.

00:15:04.831 --> 00:15:06.723
The third is duplicate payments.

00:15:07.073 --> 00:15:11.125
Before you click Approve, cross reference the invoice number in the system.

00:15:11.475 --> 00:15:15.681
It's the same discipline that caught Riverton Spine and Rehab's double billed May

00:15:15.681 --> 00:15:16.200
4th visit.

00:15:16.550 --> 00:15:19.929
A few seconds of checking can save hours of clawing money back.

00:15:22.479 --> 00:15:23.477
The money is out.

00:15:23.827 --> 00:15:25.220
Can we close the file?

00:15:25.570 --> 00:15:26.290
Not yet.

00:15:26.640 --> 00:15:31.514
Before any case is marked Archived, the case manager confirms that the key document

00:15:31.514 --> 00:15:32.747
sets are in the file.

00:15:33.097 --> 00:15:35.430
The first is the net sheet, the money trail.

00:15:35.780 --> 00:15:38.311
It's the final accounting of every dollar that moved.

00:15:38.661 --> 00:15:40.705
Behind it, you need three things.

00:15:41.055 --> 00:15:44.793
An itemized list of every check, ACH or wire transfer.

00:15:45.143 --> 00:15:48.957
On our file, that starts with the three carrier checks:

00:15:48.957 --> 00:15:52.494
Keystone's check number 0048213 for 10,000 dollars,

00:15:52.494 --> 00:15:55.337
then Harbor Point's and Cardinal State's.

00:15:55.687 --> 00:16:00.593
You also need the transaction IDs, the bank confirmation numbers for each payment,

00:16:00.593 --> 00:16:02.328
so any payment can be traced.

00:16:02.678 --> 00:16:07.358
And a zero balance confirmation, a report showing the approved budget minus the

00:16:07.358 --> 00:16:09.017
total disbursed equals zero.

00:16:09.367 --> 00:16:13.366
That zero tells a later reviewer that nothing is outstanding and the remaining

00:16:13.366 --> 00:16:14.905
balance has been de obligated.

00:16:15.255 --> 00:16:20.413
For John, the trust ledger reached zero dollars after the October 22nd,

00:16:20.413 --> 00:16:21.721
2026 disbursement.

00:16:22.071 --> 00:16:25.368
If you don't get to zero, the case isn't ready to close.

00:16:25.718 --> 00:16:27.146
Find the difference first.

00:16:29.696 --> 00:16:31.751
The net sheet shows where the money went.

00:16:32.101 --> 00:16:35.933
The next set proves it got there and did what it was meant to do.

00:16:36.283 --> 00:16:40.811
That's proof of delivery or completion, evidence the funds were used for their

00:16:40.811 --> 00:16:41.797
intended purpose.

00:16:42.147 --> 00:16:46.379
The most basic piece is a signed acknowledgment of receipt,

00:16:46.379 --> 00:16:51.830
John's own signature confirming he received his 20,359 dollars and 92 cents.

00:16:52.180 --> 00:16:55.094
That protects the firm if questions come up later.

00:16:55.444 --> 00:16:58.287
If a disbursement paid for construction or repairs,

00:16:58.287 --> 00:17:02.747
you'd also need a signed Certificate of Completion or a final inspection report.

00:17:03.097 --> 00:17:07.661
John's settlement didn't pay for repairs, and the Civic's repair ran on Margaret's

00:17:07.661 --> 00:17:11.224
separate property damage claim, so that item doesn't apply here.

00:17:11.574 --> 00:17:14.876
And when you pay providers, you need provider lien waivers,

00:17:14.876 --> 00:17:19.187
signed documents saying they've been paid in full and waive any future claims

00:17:19.187 --> 00:17:20.978
against the client or the claim.

00:17:21.328 --> 00:17:26.797
On our file, that's Riverton Spine and Rehab at its reduced 4,580 dollars,

00:17:26.797 --> 00:17:31.823
and Peach State's 1,240 dollars needs the same written confirmation.

00:17:32.173 --> 00:17:34.542
This ties back to the final payoff letter.

00:17:34.892 --> 00:17:36.668
Paying a provider isn't enough.

00:17:37.018 --> 00:17:38.968
You need it in writing that they're done.

00:17:41.518 --> 00:17:44.630
The next set is compliance and identity verification.

00:17:44.980 --> 00:17:48.916
In other words, proving on paper that we paid the right people the right way.

00:17:49.266 --> 00:17:50.577
It has two items.

00:17:50.927 --> 00:17:56.388
First, updated W-9 and tax documents, so the most recent tax information is on file

00:17:56.388 --> 00:17:58.625
for any ten ninety-nine reporting.

00:17:58.975 --> 00:18:01.889
Check that what's in the file is current before you close it.

00:18:02.239 --> 00:18:05.420
Second, the duplication of benefits final review.

00:18:05.770 --> 00:18:09.848
This is a signed statement from the beneficiary confirming he didn't receive double

00:18:09.848 --> 00:18:13.189
funding from another source, like insurance, after the case started.

00:18:13.539 --> 00:18:15.478
Why does that matter on John's file?

00:18:15.828 --> 00:18:18.556
Because his care was paid from several directions.

00:18:18.906 --> 00:18:22.125
Harbor Point's PIP paid 5,000 dollars of bills,

00:18:22.125 --> 00:18:27.741
Peach State paid the injection, and Riverton Spine and Rehab treated under an LOP.

00:18:28.091 --> 00:18:32.468
With that many payers, the file has to show that no expense was paid twice.

00:18:32.818 --> 00:18:34.885
It's the same principle as the net sheet.

00:18:35.235 --> 00:18:39.275
Every dollar should come from one clear source, and the file should prove it.

00:18:41.825 --> 00:18:45.726
Next is the document people tend to skip, the internal closing memo.

00:18:46.076 --> 00:18:49.120
It's a one page summary you write for the internal file,

00:18:49.120 --> 00:18:53.251
and it's the first thing anyone reads if they open this case years from now.

00:18:53.601 --> 00:18:57.920
John's closing memo was dated October 30th, 2026.

00:18:58.270 --> 00:19:00.023
It should cover three things.

00:19:00.373 --> 00:19:03.693
First, a case summary, how it started versus how it ended.

00:19:04.043 --> 00:19:09.068
For John, that's a file that opened with a 14 day treatment gap and a 3,500 dollar

00:19:09.068 --> 00:19:13.235
opening offer from Keystone, and ended with all three policy limits,

00:19:13.235 --> 00:19:14.829
40,000 dollars, recovered.

00:19:15.179 --> 00:19:17.060
Second, discrepancy notes.

00:19:17.410 --> 00:19:21.194
If the final payout differs from the first estimate, explain why.

00:19:21.544 --> 00:19:24.319
The slide's example about a change order is general.

00:19:24.669 --> 00:19:31.597
On our file, you'd explain why Riverton Spine and Rehab's claimed 6,290 dollars

00:19:31.597 --> 00:19:36.244
became 6,105 after audit and 4,580 after negotiation.

00:19:36.594 --> 00:19:41.006
Third, exception reports, documenting any policy waivers granted along the way.

00:19:41.356 --> 00:19:44.958
If a rule was bent for a good reason, write the reason down now,

00:19:44.958 --> 00:19:46.534
while you still remember it.

00:19:49.084 --> 00:19:53.078
The last piece of reconciliation is the retention and disposition schedule.

00:19:53.428 --> 00:19:57.224
Once a case closes, the paperwork doesn't just sit forever.

00:19:57.574 --> 00:20:02.413
It's organized under a retention policy, and each document type has its own timeline

00:20:02.413 --> 00:20:03.623
and its own end step.

00:20:03.973 --> 00:20:06.353
The slide's table is a general example.

00:20:06.703 --> 00:20:11.254
Financial records are kept 7 years as the standard and then digitally deleted.

00:20:11.604 --> 00:20:16.237
Eligibility documents are kept 3 to 5 years and then archived or moved off site.

00:20:16.586 --> 00:20:20.673
Correspondence is kept 3 years and then purged from the active server.

00:20:21.023 --> 00:20:25.822
Our training file follows the firm's schedule: the closed file is kept 7 years from

00:20:25.822 --> 00:20:29.696
closing, so don't apply the slide's shorter periods to John's file.

00:20:30.046 --> 00:20:31.602
Why does this matter to you?

00:20:31.952 --> 00:20:36.408
Because how you file things at closing decides whether the right documents can be

00:20:36.408 --> 00:20:40.369
found while they must be kept, and cleared out properly once they don't.

00:20:40.719 --> 00:20:44.538
Filing it right at closing is the final step in handling a case well.

00:20:47.088 --> 00:20:49.341
That brings us to the end of Day 3.

00:20:49.691 --> 00:20:54.280
Today we learned how to protect the client's settlement through lien negotiations,

00:20:54.280 --> 00:20:58.421
using legal doctrines, billing audits, and a combined BI and UM net sheet.

00:20:58.771 --> 00:21:01.242
Then we followed that money through disbursement,

00:21:01.242 --> 00:21:05.476
from the workflow and the documentation packet to the final reconciliation that lets

00:21:05.476 --> 00:21:06.434
a case be archived.

00:21:06.784 --> 00:21:09.850
Review your notes on John Doe versus Robert Smith tonight,

00:21:09.850 --> 00:21:14.079
because these are the steps that make sure the client keeps every dollar they're

00:21:14.079 --> 00:21:14.714
entitled to.

00:21:15.063 --> 00:21:15.702
Thank you.

